\[
ewcommand{\im}{\mathrm{i}\mkern1mu} 
ewcommand{ s}{ oldsymbol} 
ewcommand{\cl}{\mathcal} 
ewcommand{\ud}{\mathrm{d}} 
ewcommand{\Rbb}{\mathbb{R}} 
ewcommand{\Cbb}{\mathbb{C}} 
ewcommand{	inv}[1]{ rac{1}{#1}}\] <blockquote> <p><strong>Definition. (Moreau envelope)</strong> Let $f: \Rbb^n ightarrow (-\infty, +\infty]$ be a proper, lower semi-continuous, convex function. For a parameter $\lambda &gt; 0$, the <strong>Moreau envelope</strong> of $f$ with index $\lambda$ is defined as</p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:moreau} \mathrm{M}_\lambda f(x) = \inf_{y\in \Rbb^n} \left\{ f(y) + 	inv{2\lambda} \| x-y \|_2^2 ight\}. \end{equation}\] <p>This can be seen as a “<em>smoothed</em>” version of $f$, obtained by infimal convolution with the squared norm. Closely related is the <strong>proximal operator</strong>:</p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:prox} \mathrm{prox}_{\lambda f} (x) := \mathrm{argmin}_{y \in \Rbb^n}  ig\{ f(y) + 	inv{2\lambda} \| x-y \|_2^2  ig\}. \end{equation}\] </blockquote> <p>From \eqref{eq:moreau} and \eqref{eq:prox}, the Moreau enveloppe can be expressed as:</p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:3} \mathrm{M}_\lambda f(x) = f(\mathrm{prox}_{\lambda f} (x)) + 	inv{2\lambda} \| x-\mathrm{prox}_{\lambda f} (x) \|_2^2. \end{equation}\] <h2 id="example-the-absolute-value">Example: the absolute value:</h2> <table> <tbody> <tr> <td>Particularizing \eqref{eq:moreau} to $$f(x)=</td> <td>x</td> <td>$$ yields</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> \[ egin{align} \mathrm{prox}_{\lambda|\cdot|} (x) &amp;= \mathrm{argmin}_{y\in \Rbb^n} \left\{ |y| + 	inv{2 \lambda} (x-y)^2 ight\} \ &amp;= \mathrm{argmin}_{y\in \Rbb^n}  egin{cases} &amp;y + 	inv{2 \lambda} (x-y)^2,~~y&gt;0 \ &amp;-y + 	inv{2 \lambda} (x-y)^2,~~y&lt;0 \ \end{cases} \ &amp;\Leftrightarrow  egin{cases} &amp;\lambda + y-x = 0,~~y&gt;0 \ &amp;-\lambda + y-x = 0,~~y&lt;0 \ \end{cases} \ &amp;\Leftrightarrow  egin{cases} &amp;y = x-\lambda,~~x&gt;\lambda \ &amp;y = x+\lambda,~~x&lt;-\lambda \ &amp;y=0,~~\mathrm{otherwise.} \end{cases} \label{eq:7}. \end{align}\] <p>The solution in \eqref{eq:7} can be synthesized as:</p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:soft} \mathrm{prox}_{\lambda|\cdot|} (x) = \mathrm{sign}(x) \max \{ |x| - \lambda, 0 \}. \end{equation}\] <p>The result in \eqref{eq:soft} is the 1-D case of the well-known <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proximal_gradient_methods_for_learning#Solving_for_L1_proximity_operator"><em>Soft-thresholding</em> operator</a> \(\cl S_\lambda = \mathrm{prox}_{\lambda \| \cdot \|_1}\).</p> <center> <img src="/assets/img/posts/soft_thresholding.png" alt="Moreau envelope" width="420px"/> <img src="/assets/img/posts/moreau_envelope.png" alt="Moreau envelope" width="420px"/> </center> <div class="caption"> Fig. 1: (left) Moreau envelope of the absolute value function. (right) Soft-thresholding operator. </div> <p>Finally, by injecting \eqref{eq:soft} into \eqref{eq:3}, the Moreau envelope of the absolute value function can be written as</p> \[ egin{align} \mathrm{M}_\lambda |x| &amp;= |\mathrm{sign}(x) \max\{ |x|-\lambda,0 \}| + 	inv{2\lambda} (x-\mathrm{sign}(x) \max\{ |x|-\lambda,0 \})^2 \ &amp;=  egin{cases} &amp;	inv{2\lambda} x^2,~~|x|\le \lambda, \ &amp;x- rac{\lambda}{2},~~x&gt;\lambda \ &amp;-x- rac{\lambda}{2},~~x&lt;-\lambda \ \end{cases} \ &amp;=  egin{cases} &amp;	inv{2\lambda} x^2,~~|x|\le \lambda, \ &amp;|x|- rac{\lambda}{2},~~|x|&gt; \lambda, \ \end{cases}. \end{align}\] <hr/> <p>The <em>soft-thresholding</em> operator is of great interest because it is often involved in minimization problems of the form</p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:inv_prob} 	ilde{ s x} = \mathrm{argmin}_{ s x} 	inv 2 \|  s y -  s{Ax} \|_2^2 + \lambda \|  s x \|_1, \end{equation}\] <p>where \(\lambda \|  s x \|_1\) is a <em>convex</em> but <em>nondifferentiable</em> function so that we cannot compute their gradient. Eq. \eqref{eq:inv*prob} can be solved efficiently by the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proximal_gradient_methods_for_learning"><em>proximal gradient method</em> (PGM)</a> that consists of alternating because a gradient descent step with respect to the differentiable function and a <em>proximal</em> step with respect to the nondifferentiable function(s). For instance, \eqref{eq:inv_prob} can be solved by the iterations</p> \[ egin{equation}  s x^{(k+1)} = \cl S_\lambda  ig( s x^{(k)} - \alpha  s A^* ( s{Ax}^{(k)} -  s y)  ig). \end{equation}\]

\[
ewcommand{\im}{\mathrm{i}} 
ewcommand{ s}{ oldsymbol} 
ewcommand{\cl}{\mathcal} 
ewcommand{\ud}{\mathrm{d}} 
ewcommand{\Rbb}{\mathbb{R}} 
ewcommand{\Cbb}{\mathbb{C}} 
ewcommand{	inv}[1]{ rac{1}{#1}} 
ewcommand{\intfty}{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}} 
ewcommand{\iintfty}{\iint_{-\infty}^{\infty}} 
ewcommand{\so}{\sin (	heta)} 
ewcommand{\co}{\cos (	heta)}\] <blockquote> <p><strong>Definition. (Radon transform)</strong> The Radon transform \(\cl R \{f\}\) of $f: \Rbb^2 ightarrow \Rbb$ is</p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:radon} \cl R \{f\}(t, 	heta) = \iintfty f(x,y) \delta(t-x \co - y \so) \ud x \ud y, \end{equation}\] <p>for $t \in \Rbb$ and $0\le 	heta &lt; \pi$.</p> </blockquote> <center> <img src="/assets/img/posts/fourier_slice_theorem.png" alt="fourier-slice" width="500px"/> </center> <div class="caption"> Fig. 1: Illustration of the Fourier-slice theorem. </div> <p>We wish to prove the <em>Fourier-Slice Theorem</em> reminded in p.37 of <a class="citation" href="#adcock">(Adcock &amp; Hansen, 2021)</a></p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:fourier_slice} \cl{F}_1  ig\{ \cl R \{ f \}  ig\} (\omega, 	heta) = \cl F \{ f \} (\omega \co, \omega \so),~~~~\omega \in \Rbb,~ 	heta \in [0,2\pi) \end{equation}\] <p>Where $\cl F$ denotes the 2-D Fourier transform and $\cl F_1$ is the 1-D Fourier transform with respect to the first component.</p> <p><em>proof</em>.</p> <p>Using \eqref{eq:radon} and the definition of the Fourier transform, the left term of \eqref{eq:fourier_slice} writes as</p> \[ egin{align} \label{eq:left} \cl{F}_1  ig\{ \cl R \{ f \}  ig\} (\omega, 	heta) &amp;= \intfty \left( \iintfty f(x,y) \delta(t-x \co -y \so) \ud x \ud y ight) e^{-\im \omega t} \ud t \ &amp;= \iintfty f(x,y)  ig( \intfty \delta(t-x \co -y \so) e^{-\im \omega t} \ud t  ig) \ud x \ud y \ &amp;= \iintfty f(x,y)  ig( \intfty \delta(t-x \co -y \so) e^{-\im \omega (x \co+y\so)} \ud t  ig) \ud x \ud y \ &amp;= \iintfty f(x,y) e^{-\im \omega (x \co+y\so)} \underbrace{ ig(\intfty \delta(t-x \co -y \so) \ud t  ig)}_{=1} \ud x \ud y \ &amp;= \iintfty f(x,y) e^{-\im \omega (x \co+y\so)} \ud x \ud y \ &amp;= \cl F \{ f \} (\omega \co, \omega \so) \end{align}\] <p>which proves \eqref{eq:fourier_slice}. □</p> <h2 id="references">References</h2> <h2 class="bibliography">2021</h2> <ol class="bibliography"><li><div class="row"> <div class="col col-sm-2 abbr"> </div> <div id="adcock" class="col-sm-8"> <div class="title">Compressive imaging: structure, sampling, learning</div> <div class="author"> Ben Adcock and Anders C Hansen </div> <div class="periodical"> 2021 </div> <div class="periodical"> </div> <div class="links"> </div> </div> </div> </li></ol>

\[
ewcommand{ s}{ oldsymbol} 
ewcommand{\ud}{\mathrm{d}} 
ewcommand{\Rbb}{\mathbb{R}} 
ewcommand{\Cbb}{\mathbb{C}} 
ewcommand{	inv}[1]{ rac{1}{#1}} 
ewcommand{\indep}{\perp \!\! \perp}\] <p>In the following derivations, we show that the typical regularised inverse problem to recover a quantity $ s x \in \Rbb^N$ from noisy observations of the form $ s y =  s{Hx} +  s n \in \Cbb^M$ with additive noise $ s n \in \Cbb^M$ corresponds to the <em>maximum a posteriori</em> (MAP) estimator when a prior distribution \(p( s x) = e^{-	heta \|  s{\Psi x} \|_1}\) is assumed on the data:</p> \[ egin{align} \hat x_{	ext{MAP}} &amp;= \mathrm{argmax}_{ s x}~ p( s x| s y) = \mathrm{argmax}_{ s x}~  rac{p( s y| s x) p( s x)}{p( s y)} \underset{p( s y) \indep  s x}{=} \mathrm{argmax}_{ s x}~ p( s y| s x) p( s x) \ &amp;= \mathrm{argmax}_{ s x}~ e^{-	inv 2 \| s y- s{Hx}\|_2^2} e^{-	heta \| s{\Psi x}\|_1} = \mathrm{argmax}_{ s x}~ e^{-	inv 2 \| s y- s{Hx}\|_2^2 -	heta \| s{\Psi x}\|_1} \ &amp;= \mathrm{argmax}_{ s x}~ \log  ig(e^{-	inv 2 \| s y- s{Hx}\|_2^2 -	heta \| s{\Psi x}\|_1}  ig) \ &amp;= \mathrm{argmin}_{ s x}~ \underbrace{	inv 2 \| s y- s{Hx}\|_2^2}_{	ext{Data fidelity}} + \underbrace{	heta \| s{\Psi x}\|_1}_{	ext{Regularization}}. \end{align}\] <p>Additionnally, we define <em>Bayesian point estimators</em> that arise from the decision ``what point $\hat{ s x} \in \Rbb^N$ summarises $ s x| s y$ best?’’. The optimal decision under uncertainty is</p> \[ egin{equation*} \hat{ s x}_L = \mathrm{argmin}_{ s u \in \Rbb^N}~ \mathbb{E}[L( s u, s x)| s y] = \mathrm{argmin}_{ s u \in \Rbb^N}~ \int L( s u, s x) p( s x| s y) \ud  s x \end{equation*}\] <p>where the loss $L( s u, s x)$ measures the “dissimilarity” between $ s u$ and $ s x$.</p> <p>General desiderata:</p> <ul> <li>$L( s u, s x) \ge 0,~  orall  s u,  s x \in \Rbb^N$,</li> <li>$L( s u, s x)=0 \Leftrightarrow  s u= s x$,</li> <li>$L$ strictly convex w.r.t. $ s u$ (for estimator uniqueness).</li> </ul> <p><strong><u>Example:</u></strong></p> <p>The <em>minimum mean square error</em> (MMSE) estimator, taking \(L( s u, s x) = \| s u- s x\|_2^2\), is obtained as:</p> \[ egin{align*}  egin{split} \hat{ s x}_{	ext{MMSE}} &amp;= \mathrm{argmin}_{ s u}~ \int \| s u- s x\|_2^2 p( s x| s y) \ud  s x \ &amp;\Leftrightarrow \int (\hat{ s x}_{	ext{MMSE}}- s x) p( s x| s y) \ud  s x=0 \ &amp;\Leftrightarrow \hat{ s x}_{	ext{MMSE}} \underbrace{\int p( s x| s y) \ud  s x}_{=1} = \int  s x p( s x| s y) \ud  s x \ &amp;\Leftrightarrow \hat{ s x}_{	ext{MMSE}} = \mathbb{E}[ s x| s y]. \end{split} \end{align*}\]

\[
ewcommand{ s}{ oldsymbol} 
ewcommand{\Cbb}{\mathbb{C}} 
ewcommand{\im}{\mathrm{i}\mkern1mu} 
ewcommand{\diag}{\mathrm{diag}}\] <blockquote> <p><strong>Theorem.</strong> A circulant matrix $ s C \in \Cbb^{N	imes N}$ which has been generated using the DFT $ s u =  s{Fv} \in \Cbb^N$ (with $ s F$ the DFT matrix) of a $K$-sparse vector $ s v \in \Cbb^N$ is rank-$K$.</p> </blockquote> <p><strong>Proof.</strong> If $ s v$ is $K$-sparse (</p> \[\| s v\|_0 \le K\] <p>), it can be written as</p> <p> egin{equation} \label{eq:sparse}  s v = \sum_{q=0}^{K-1} ho_q  s e_{p_q}. \end{equation}</p> <p>Moreover, let $T( s u)$ be the operator that turns a vector $ s u \in \Cbb^N$ into a circulant matrix, that is, in <em>modulo arithmetic</em> ($u_{k-l} = u_{/k-l/_N}$)</p> \[(T( s u))_{k,l} = u_{k-l}\] <p>We can write $ s C = T( s u) = T( s{Fv})$. We also have $u_a = \sum_{b=0}^{N-1} F_{a,b} v_b$. The circulant matrix at index $jk$ can thus write</p> \[ egin{align} \label{eq:dev}  egin{split} C_{jk} &amp;= u_{j-k} = \sum_{l=0}^{N-1} F_{j-k,l}~ v_l \underset{\eqref{eq:sparse}}{=} \sum_{l=0}^{N-1} F_{j-k,l} \sum_{q=0}^{K-1} ho_q \delta_{l,p_q} \ &amp;= \sum_{q=0}^{K-1} ho_q F_{j-k,p_q } \underbrace{\sum_{l=0}^{N-1} \delta_{l,p_q}}_{=1} \ &amp;= \sum_{q=0}^{K-1} ho_q F_{j,p_q} F_{k,p_q}^* \end{split} \end{align}\] <p>where the last line in \eqref{eq:dev} is obtained thanks to the definition of the coefficients of the DFT matrix $F_{jk} = e^{ rac{-\im 2\pi jk}{N}}$. This is the critical property which allows this proof. Finally, the circulant matrix writes as</p> <p> egin{equation} \label{eq:not1}  s C = \sum_{q=0}^{K-1} ho_q  s f[p_q]  s f[p_q]^* \end{equation}</p> <p>with $ s f[n] :=  s F_{:,n}$, which makes it rank-$K$.</p> <p>The circulant matrix $ s C$ in \eqref{eq:not1} can also be equivalently represented as</p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:quasiSVD}  s C =  egin{bmatrix} &amp; &amp; &amp; \ &amp; &amp; &amp; \  s f [p_0] &amp;  s f [p_1] &amp; \dots \ &amp; &amp; &amp; \ &amp; &amp; &amp; \end{bmatrix}  egin{bmatrix} ho_0 &amp; &amp; &amp; \ &amp; ho_1 &amp; &amp; \ &amp; &amp; ho_2 &amp; \ &amp; &amp; &amp; \ddots \end{bmatrix}  egin{bmatrix} &amp; &amp;  s f^*[p_0] &amp; &amp; \ &amp; &amp;  s f^*[p_1] &amp; &amp; \ &amp; &amp; \vdots &amp; &amp; \ \end{bmatrix} =  s{FP} \diag( s ho)  s P^*  s F^*, \end{equation}\] <p>with $ s ho \in \mathbb{R}^K$ containing the $K$ coefficients of the vector $ s v$ which also shows $ s C$ is rank-$K$.</p> <ul>Note:</ul> <p>With the conventional writing $ s v = \sum_{l=0}^{N-1} v_l  s e_l$, a derivation similar to \eqref{eq:dev} would lead to $ s C = \sum_{l=0}^{N-1} v_l  s f[l]  s f[l]^*$.</p> <p>Thus</p> <p> egin{equation} \label{eq:decomp}  s C =  s F \diag(  s v)  s F^*<br/> \end{equation}</p> <p>We see that \eqref{eq:quasiSVD} is very close to the SVD definition:</p> \[ s C :=  s{U\Sigma V}^*.\] <p>The subtlety comes where the singular values in $ s\Sigma$ are:</p> <ul> <li><strong>all positive</strong>: this implies \( s C =  s F  s P |\diag( s ho)|  s P^* 	ilde{ s F}^*\) where $	ilde{ s F}^*$ is the Fourier matrix with each column $i$ multiplied by $+1$ or $-1$ depending on the sign of associated $ho_i$.</li> <li><strong>aranged in descending order</strong>: this implies the selection matrix $ s P$ must also contain permutation to ensure $ sho$ is aranged in descending order.</li> </ul> <p>To synthetize, we have</p> \[ egin{align}  egin{split}  s C &amp;=  s F \diag ( s v)  s F^* \ &amp;=  s F \diag(| s v|)  s S  s F^*\ &amp;=  s F  s P^* \diag(|	ilde{ s v}|)  s{PSF}^* \ &amp;=  s{U\Sigma V}^*, \end{split} \end{align}\] <p>with $ s S = \diag(	ext{sign}( s v))$, $ s P$ is a permutation matrix such that</p> \[ s P | s v| = |	ilde{ s v}|\] <p>and</p> \[	ilde{v}_k \ge |	ilde{v}_{k+1}|\] <table> <tbody> <tr> <td>, $ s \Sigma= \diag(</td> <td>	ilde{ s v}</td> <td>)$, and the unitary matrices</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> \[ s U =  s F  s P^*\] <p>, and $ s V =  s F  s S  s P$. By definition, this means that</p> \[ s U  s \Sigma  s V^*\] <p>is the SVD decomposition of $ s C$.</p> <p>In fact,</p> \[ s{U\Sigma V}^* =  s U  s Z  s \Sigma  s Z^*  s V^*\] <p>for any $ s Z = {m diag}( s z)$ such that $ s Z  s Z^* =  s I$ (that is, $ s z$ is such that $|z_k|=1$). So we can choose $ s z$ to make $ s U s Z$ and $ s V  s Z$ real, as in J. Romberg developments.</p> <p>This proves a rank-$K$ circulant matrix contains only $K$ degrees of freedom, very less than a general rank-$K$ matrix containing $(2K+1)N$ DOFs.</p> <p>We also see a circulant matrix is hermitian if $ s v \in \mathbb{R}^N$ as $ s C^* =  s F \diag( s v)^*  s F^* =  s F \diag( s v)  s F^* =  s C \Leftrightarrow  s v =  s v^*$.</p>

\[
ewcommand{ s}{ oldsymbol} 
ewcommand{\cl}{\mathcal} 
ewcommand{	inv}[1]{ rac{1}{#1}} 
ewcommand{\Cbb}{\mathbb{C}} 
ewcommand{\Rbb}{\mathbb{R}} 
ewcommand{\diag}{\mathrm{diag}} 
ewcommand{\upto}[1]{\llbracket #1 rbracket}\] <p>In the aim to compute an approximate solution to the linear inverse problem $ s{Ax}= s y$ with $ s A \in \Rbb^{n	imes n}$ of rank $r \leq n$, one is interested in evaluating the loss</p> \[\cl L ( s x^{(k)},  s y) = 	inv 2 \| s A  s x^{(k)} - s y\|_2^2\] <p>when $ s x^{(k)}$ is the $k$-th iterate of the <em>Gradient Descent</em> (GD) algorithm with zero initialization. It writes $ s x^{(k)} = \sum_{i=0}^k ( s I-\alpha  s A^*  s A)^i \alpha  s A^*  s y$, and writing the SVD decomposition</p> \[ s A :=  s U  s \Sigma  s V^*\] <p>with the properties $ s V^*  s V =  s I_r$ and $ s U^*  s U =  s I_r$, one gets</p> \[ egin{align*}  s A  s x^{(k)} &amp;= \sum_{i=0}^k  s U  s \Sigma  s V^* ( s I_n - \alpha  s V  s\Sigma^2  s V^*) ( s I_n - \alpha  s A^*  s A)^{i-1} \alpha  s A^*  s y \ &amp;= \sum_{i=0}^k  s U  s \Sigma ( s I_r - \alpha  s\Sigma^2)  s V^* ( s I_n - \alpha  s A^*  s A)^{i-1} \alpha  s A^*  s y \ &amp;= \sum_{i=0}^k  s U  s \Sigma ( s I_r - \alpha  s\Sigma^2)^i  s V^* \alpha  s A^*  s y \ &amp;=  s U  s\Sigma \underbrace{\sum_{i=0}^k ( s I_r - \alpha  s\Sigma^2)^i}_{(\alpha  s\Sigma^2)^{-1} ( s I_r - ( s I_r - \alpha  s\Sigma^2)^{k+1})} \alpha  s\Sigma  s U^*  s y \ &amp;=  s U  s\Sigma^{-1} ( s I_r - ( s I_r - \alpha  s\Sigma^2)^{k+1})  s\Sigma  s U^*  s y \ &amp;=  s U ( s I_r - ( s I_r - \alpha  s\Sigma^2)^{k+1})  s U^*  s y. \end{align*}\] <p>Hence, with \(\alpha 	riangleq  rac{\eta}{\| s A\|^2}\),</p> \[ egin{align} \label{eq:loss}  egin{split} \cl L ( s x^{(k)},  s y) &amp;= 	inv 2  igg\|\underbrace{( s U  s U^* -  s I_n)  s y}_{\perp 	ext{Im }  s U } -  s U \diag  igg( 1-\eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_i}{\sigma_{\mathrm{max}}}  ig)^2  igg)^{k+1}  s U^*  s y  igg\|_2^2 \ &amp;= 	inv 2  igg\| ( s U  s U^* -  s I_n)  s y  igg\|_2^2 + 	inv 2  igg\|  s U \diag  igg( 1-\eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_i}{\sigma_{m max}}  ig)^2  igg)^{k+1}  s U^*  s y  igg\|_2^2 \ &amp;= 	inv 2  igg\| ( s U  s U^* -  s I_n)  s y  igg\|_2^2 + 	inv 2  igg\| \diag  igg( 1-\eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_i}{\sigma_{m max}}  ig)^2  igg)^{k+1}  s U^*  s y  igg\|_2^2 \ &amp;\leq 	inv 2  igg\| ( s U  s U^* -  s I_n)  s y  igg\|_2^2 + 	inv 2 \max_{i \in \upto{r}}  igg| 1- \eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_i}{\sigma_{m max}}  ig)^2  igg|^{2(k+1)}  ig\|  s U^*  s y  ig\|_2^2. \end{split} \end{align}\] <p>The third line is obtained from the second line by using the identity</p> \[\| s{UM} \|_2^2=\langle  s U  s M,  s U  s M angle = \langle  s U^*  s U  s M,  s M angle = \langle  s M,  s M angle=\| s M \|_2^2.\] <p>By convention $\sigma_{\mathrm max} = \sigma_1$. In \eqref{eq:loss}, the first error term is independent of $k$. One cannot do better than targetting the fastest convergence to zero of the second term. For a fixed stepsize $\eta$, the best choice must satisfy</p> \[ egin{align*} \min_\eta \max_{i \in \upto{r} }  igg| 1- \eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_i}{\sigma_{m max}}  ig)^2  igg| &amp;= \min_\eta \max  igg( \max_{i \in \upto{r} } 1- \eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_i}{\sigma_{m max}}  ig)^2, \max_{i \in \upto{r} } \eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_i}{\sigma_{m max}}  ig)^2 -1  igg) \ &amp;= \min_\eta \max  igg( 1- \eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_r}{\sigma_{m max}}  ig)^2,\eta -1  igg) \end{align*}\] <p>which is reached when</p> \[ egin{align} \label{eq:eta}  egin{split} &amp;1 - \eta  ig(  rac{\sigma_r}{\sigma_{m max}}  ig)^2 = \eta - 1 \ \Leftrightarrow ~&amp; \eta =  rac{2}{1+(\sigma_r/\sigma_1)^2}. \end{split} \end{align}\] <p>One observes that the ideal constant stepsize of the GD method depends only on the ratio between the smallest and the largest singular value which can be associated to the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Condition_number">condition number</a></p> \[ egin{equation} \label{eq:condition_number} \kappa( s A) :=  rac{\sigma_1( s A)}{\sigma_r ( s A)}. \end{equation}\] <p>In particular, injecting \eqref{eq:eta} into \eqref{eq:loss} yields</p> \[ egin{align*} \cl L_k ( s x^{(k)},  s y) &amp;\leq 	inv 2  igg\| ( s U  s U^* -  s I_n)  s y  igg\|_2^2 + 	inv 2  ig| \eta - 1  ig|^{2(k+1)}  ig\|  s U^*  s y  ig\|_2^2 \ &amp;= 	inv 2  igg\| ( s U  s U^* -  s I_n)  s y  igg\|_2^2 + 	inv 2  igg|  rac{1-(\sigma_r/\sigma_1)^2 }{1+(\sigma_r/\sigma_1)^2}  igg|^{2(k+1)}  ig\|  s U^*  s y  ig\|_2^2, \end{align*}\] <p>meaning that the decreasing of the loss with the iterations of the gradient descent is slower when $\sigma_r \ll \sigma_1$.</p> <center> <img src="/assets/img/posts/ideal_stepsize.png" alt="Gradient Descent iterations" width="500px"/> </center> <p><strong>Figure 1</strong>: Gradient Descent iterations as a function of the stepsize for the system $ s A  s x =  s y$ with $ s A \in \Rbb^{30	imes 30}$ of rank $r=5$.<br/> In this experiment, the ideal choice following \eqref{eq:eta} was $\eta^\star = 1.26$.</p>
